I reviewed the actual updated PR #2 as well. I would still not merge yet, because the deeper relational review exposed one important transaction-identity issue that becomes especially relevant once we preserve P2QPK witness data.

The major remaining issue: TXID is not enough to identify witness data

Qogecoin Core maintains two different hashes for a witness transaction. GetHash() computes the traditional txid without witness, while GetWitnessHash() hashes the complete transaction including witness. Core's JSON output exposes them separately as:

txid -> GetHash()
hash -> GetWitnessHash()

That means two transaction serializations can have the same txid but different witnesses and therefore different wtxids.

The current schema cannot represent that faithfully. transactions is keyed only by txid and stores size, vsize, and weight; block_transactions identifies occurrences only by txid; and transaction_input_witness is also keyed only by (txid, vin_index, item_index).

So consider competing blocks:

Block A:
  txid = T
  wtxid = W1
  witness = signature A

Block B:
  txid = T
  wtxid = W2
  witness = signature B

Both share the same non-witness transaction body, but the database currently has room for only one witness stack and one set of size/weight values for T.

That contradicts the new goal of retaining orphan blocks as a complete audit trail. It is particularly relevant to P2QPK because the 17,088-byte SLH-DSA signature is entirely witness data.

Phase 2A's Go model also currently has only TxID; there is no WTxID. This should be corrected now, before RPC translation and indexing are written.

I found three smaller relational gaps too

The improved utxo_state proves that a spending input exists, but it does not yet prove that the input actually spends this particular UTXO. For example, input S:0 could spend output A:3, while utxo_state could incorrectly claim that S:0 spent B:7; all current FKs would still be satisfiable. We can enforce the exact prevout relationship with one composite FK.

The new witness-metadata constraint is much better, but unknown_witness currently accepts any non-null program with version >0—including v1/32, which should be p2tr, v2/32, which should be p2qpk, and program lengths outside the witness-program 2–40 byte range. Similarly, the v0 unknown branch should restrict its preserved witness program to 2–40 bytes.

Finally, the migration checksum mechanism checks only UpSQL. The code explicitly allows DownSQL to change unchecked, even though Down() will later execute that changed SQL. It also intentionally ignores an applied migration whose local definition has disappeared. For an append-only migration history, both should be treated as drift.

